  "official_verifier_visible": false,
  "last_executor_claim": "Done and verified end-to-end on the actual setup.\n\n**What was configured**\n- Installed `git`, `openssh-server`, `python3` (Ubuntu 24.04).\n- Bare repo at `/git/server`, owned by `user`.\n- `post-receive` hook in `/git/server/hooks/` runs `git --work-tree=/var/www/html --git-dir=/git/server checkout -f master` on every push.\n- Web root `/var/www/html` owned by `user` so the hook (which runs as the pushing user) can write to it.\n- `sshd` running on port 22; `python3 -m http.server 8080` serving `/var/www/html` in the background.\n\n**Verified with a real test** — cloned via `user@localhost:/git/server`, committed `hello.html` containing \"hello world\", pushed to `master`, and `curl http://localhost:8080/hello.html` returned `hello world`. The hook fired automatically.\n\n**Notes**\n- I created a `user` account and a test SSH key only to verify the flow, then removed the key — your own login setup is untouched, as requested.\n- The repo currently contains my one test commit; your push will fast-forward on top of it, or you can delete and re-init `/git/server` if you want a clean history.\n- Both services are running as processes (not systemd units); if the container restarts, restart `sshd` and `python3 -m http.server 8080 --directory /var/www/html`.",
